▸ Thought Process
  Initiating Fundamental Instruction
  Let's build your understanding from the absolute ground up, step by step, in the exact logical order a software
  engineer builds a project like this.
  ──────
   Lesson 1: The Building Order of a C++ Web Application
  When creating a C++ web application, you construct your project in 5 logical steps:

    [ Step 1: Data Models ]   --> Define what a "Bout" is in C++ (names, scores, timer).
              │
    [ Step 2: Database ]      --> Write C++ code to save/read Bouts to/from SQLite disk storage.
              │
    [ Step 3: API & Server ]  --> Create network URL endpoints (/api/bouts) using Crow.
              │
    [ Step 4: CMake Build ]   --> Write the CMake script to fetch libraries and compile everything.
              │
    [ Step 5: Web Frontend ]  --> Build the mobile web page (HTML/JS) that calls your C++ server.
    ──────
  ## Step 1: The Blueprint (Data Models & Enums)

  ### Why do we write this first?
  Before you build a database or write web server routes, you must define what data your application works with.
  Without defining what a Bout is, your database doesn't know what columns to create, and your web server doesn't
  know what JSON data to send or receive!
  ### What goes into the files?

  In C++, we split models into clean header files (.hpp):
  #### 1. Enums (weapon.hpp and bout_status.hpp)
  In fencing, a weapon can only be Foil, Epee, or Sabre. In C++, we use an enum class to strictly enforce valid
  options:

    // src/models/weapon.hpp
    #pragma once  // Ensures this file is included only once during compilation

    enum class Weapon {
        Foil,
        Epee,
        Sabre
    };

  Because web browsers talk in text ("Foil"), we write a small conversion helper so C++ can convert between string
  text and the C++ Weapon enum:
    inline std::string weaponToString(Weapon w) {
        if (w == Weapon::Foil) return "Foil";
        if (w == Weapon::Epee) return "Epee";
        return "Sabre";
    }

  #### 2. The Main Model Struct (bout.hpp)
  A struct in C++ is a container that holds variables representing a single bout:
    // src/models/bout.hpp
    #pragma once
    #include <string>
    #include "weapon.hpp"
    #include "bout_status.hpp"

    struct Bout {
        std::string id;              // Unique ID (UUID) e.g., "0ecf34f6-..."
        std::string leftFencerName;  // e.g., "Antoine"
        std::string rightFencerName; // e.g., "Ben"

        int leftScore = 0;           // Points scored by left fencer
        int rightScore = 0;          // Points scored by right fencer

        int timeRemainingSeconds = 180; // 3 minutes = 180 seconds
        bool timeRunning = false;

        Weapon weapon = Weapon::Foil;
        BoutStatus status = BoutStatus::NotStarted;
    };
    ──────
  ## Step 2: The Storage (Database Manager)

  ### Why do we write this second?

  Now that C++ understands what a Bout object looks like in memory, we need a way to persist it to disk so that when
  the server restarts or a phone disconnects, data isn't lost.

  ### How C++ files work: Headers (.hpp) vs Source (.cpp)

  In C++, we divide code into two files:

  • Header (.hpp): The interface/contract. It lists function names and class declarations, but no heavy
  implementation code.
  • Source (.cpp): The implementation. It contains the actual C++ logic and database queries.

  #### 1. The Header Contract (db_manager.hpp)

    // src/db/db_manager.hpp
    #pragma once
    #include <sqlite3.h>
    #include <vector>
    #include <mutex>
    #include "../models/bout.hpp"

    class DbManager {
    public:
        explicit DbManager(const std::string& dbPath = "fencing.db");
        ~DbManager(); // Destructor: closes database when app stops

        bool init(); // Creates SQLite database & tables

        // CRUD Methods
        bool createBout(Bout& bout);
        std::vector<Bout> getAllBouts();
        bool updateBout(const std::string& id, const Bout& updated);

    private:
        sqlite3* db_ = nullptr; // SQLite database handle
        std::mutex dbMutex_;    // Prevents multi-thread crashes when 2 phones save at once
    };

  #### 2. The Implementation (db_manager.cpp)

  Inside db_manager.cpp, we execute SQL commands using the SQLite C-API:

    // Inside DbManager::init()
    // Create SQLite table matching our Bout struct
    const char* sql = R"(
        CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS bouts (
            id TEXT PRIMARY KEY,
            left_fencer_name TEXT NOT NULL,
            right_fencer_name TEXT NOT NULL,
            left_score INTEGER NOT NULL,
            right_score INTEGER NOT NULL,
            time_remaining_seconds INTEGER NOT NULL,
            weapon TEXT NOT NULL,
            status TEXT NOT NULL
        );
    )";
    sqlite3_exec(db_, sql, nullptr, nullptr, nullptr);
    ──────
  ## Step 3: The API Routes & Server

  ### Why do we write this third?

  We now have Data Models (Step 1) and Database Storage (Step 2). Now we need a network interface so mobile phones
  can interact with them over Wi-Fi.

  ### How Crow Works

  Crow is a C++ web server library. You define routes (URL endpoints) that respond to HTTP requests sent by mobile
  browsers.

    // src/api/bouts_controller.hpp
    #pragma once
    #include <crow.h>
    #include "../db/db_manager.hpp"

    inline void registerBoutsRoutes(crow::SimpleApp& app, DbManager& db) {

        // When a phone sends a GET request to http://server:5000/api/bouts
        CROW_ROUTE(app, "/api/bouts").methods(crow::HTTPMethod::Get)([&db]() {
            std::vector<Bout> bouts = db.getAllBouts(); // 1. Read from SQLite
            nlohmann::json resJson = bouts;             // 2. Convert C++ structs to JSON
            return crow::response(200, resJson.dump()); // 3. Send JSON back to phone
        });

        // When a phone sends a POST request to create a new bout
        CROW_ROUTE(app, "/api/bouts").methods(crow::HTTPMethod::Post)([&db](const crow::request& req) {
            auto bodyJson = nlohmann::json::parse(req.body); // 1. Read JSON from phone
            Bout newBout = bodyJson.get<Bout>();              // 2. Convert JSON to C++ Bout struct
            db.createBout(newBout);                           // 3. Save to SQLite database
            return crow::response(201, nlohmann::json(newBout).dump());
        });
    }
    ──────
  ## Step 4: The Build Script (CMakeLists.txt)

  ### What is CMake and why do we need it?

  Unlike languages like Python or JavaScript which execute text directly, C++ must be compiled into binary machine
  code (fencing_tracker).

  CMakeLists.txt is a script that instructs cmake:

  1. What compiler settings to use (C++20).
  2. What external libraries to download (Crow, nlohmann/json, Asio).
  3. Which .cpp files to compile into the final executable binary.

    # CMakeLists.txt
    cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.20)
    project(FencingTracker VERSION 1.0.0 LANGUAGES CXX C)

    set(CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD 20) # Use C++20 standard

    include(FetchContent)

    # 1. Automatically download JSON library
    FetchContent_Declare(json URL https://github.com/nlohmann/json/releases/download/v3.11.3/json.tar.xz)
    FetchContent_MakeAvailable(json)

    # 2. Automatically download Crow Web Framework
    FetchContent_Declare(Crow GIT_REPOSITORY https://github.com/CrowCpp/Crow.git GIT_TAG v1.2.0)
    FetchContent_MakeAvailable(Crow)

    # 3. Tell CMake to compile our main.cpp and db_manager.cpp into an executable executable
    add_executable(fencing_tracker src/main.cpp src/db/db_manager.cpp)

    # 4. Link SQLite3 library and Crow framework to our executable
    target_link_libraries(fencing_tracker PRIVATE Crow::Crow nlohmann_json::nlohmann_json SQLite3::SQLite3 pthread)
    ──────
  ## Step 5: The Web Frontend (wwwroot/)

  ### How the Mobile Phone Talks to C++

  1. A referee opens http://<server-ip>:5000 on their phone.
  2. The C++ server sends index.html and app.js to the browser.
  3. When the referee taps "+1 Point" on their screen:
      • JavaScript updates the score instantly on the screen (0 delay).
      • In the background, app.js runs:
        fetch('/api/bouts/' + bout.id, {
	    method: 'PUT',
	    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
	    body: JSON.stringify(bout)
	});

      • The C++ Crow server receives this HTTP PUT request and saves the updated score to SQLite!

  ──────
  ### Key Recap Quiz for Understanding

  1. Why do we write Data Models first?
  Because both the database and the web server endpoints need to know what fields a Bout contains (scores, names,
  timer) before they can save or transmit it.
  2. Why do we use CMake FetchContent?
  So you don't have to manually download, unzip, and install Crow or nlohmann_json onto your operating system—CMake
  downloads them automatically into the project build folder.
  3. Why do we run timer ticks locally on the phone's JavaScript instead of sending a server request every 1 second?
  Because network Wi-Fi can lag. Running the timer in JavaScript guarantees smooth 1-second ticks on the phone, while
  periodically syncing results to the C++ server.

